monetization_data.faq_schema:
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "@id": "https://kubaik.github.io/remote-premium-rates-skills-ai-cant-copy/#faq",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What skills actually command premium remote rates in 2026?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The skills that command premium rates are the ones that reduce uncertainty for the client: the ability to diagnose failures without physical access, to make and document decisions that align a distributed team, to design systems that tolerate high latency and unreliable networks, and to hand off work so that it continues without you. These are not new skills, but AI has made the obvious parts of coding cheap, so the premium has shifted toward judgment and communication under constraint. A developer who can do all four is rare and valuable."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I demonstrate these skills in an interview or proposal?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Bring artifacts, not claims. A one-page ADR, a runbook, a diagnostic script, and a latency budget for a real or realistic system are more convincing than a list of technologies. Walk through the reasoning: why you chose one option, what you rejected, and what you would measure to know if you were wrong. Interviewers and clients can evaluate reasoning; they cannot evaluate a claim of expertise."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I practice these skills without a production system?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. Use a local Docker Compose stack with a slow endpoint, a database, and a log collector. Introduce failures deliberately: kill a container, add network latency with tc, or make a dependency return errors. Then practice the four exercises against that stack. The constraints are simulated, but the reasoning is the same. The trap is to practice only the happy path; the value comes from the failure paths."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why not just learn more AI tools?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "AI tools are useful, but they are becoming commoditized. The premium is not in knowing how to prompt a model; it is in knowing what to build, how to verify it, and how to operate it when it fails. A developer who can use AI to accelerate the obvious parts and spend the saved time on diagnosis, design, and handoff is more valuable than one who only generates code faster. The constraint is not tool knowledge; it is the ability to work without supervision in a distributed environment."
      }
    }
  ]
}
